+// keys has no second split and its seam figure is not asked.
+// The fourth figure is a device behaviour, measured on the 24 GB card with the arm's first build: a
+// 16-half block read (intel_sub_group_block_read_us) whose pointer is 2 bytes past a 4-byte boundary
+// gives its odd lanes the cell two before their own (the even lanes their own). The mask is f16
+// [n_kv, rows], so the run of an odd row over an odd key count starts there: in every whole
+// 16-key group the row's odd keys take the mask cell of key - 2 (key 1 the row before's last cell).
+// With that read the double reference stands 4.4e-7 from the card's dumped answers in every row
+// (1,009, 1,013, 1,023 and 2,037 keys at 6 rows; as written 5.4e-4 .. 3.3e-3). The figure here is
+// what the read does to the answer against the CPU's, over the read's own answer squared - the
+// harness's nmse, which divides by the backend's output (at 77,297 keys the two outputs' squares
+// differ by 4 %: 4.13e-2 over the CPU's, 3.97e-2 over the device's, the card's figure). The cases
+// must be able to see it: every case of an odd key count more than 10 times its bound. The kernel reads such a run a key at a
+// time (flash_attn_dec27.cl, mal[]); its red build 7 block-reads it.
